WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.773 --> 00:00:08.708
Oh, hello.

00:00:08.741 --> 00:00:11.677
Technology isn't just
changing our lives.

00:00:11.710 --> 00:00:14.414
It's changing our brains.

00:00:14.447 --> 00:00:15.681
Not just how they think,

00:00:15.714 --> 00:00:17.483
but how they look.

00:00:17.516 --> 00:00:21.054
It's been shown that playing
certain video games for hours

00:00:21.087 --> 00:00:23.222
can improve your memory
for details,

00:00:23.255 --> 00:00:25.324
your ability
to navigate space

00:00:25.357 --> 00:00:26.592
in video games,

00:00:26.625 --> 00:00:28.394
and can make your brain--

00:00:28.427 --> 00:00:31.230
well, certain parts
of it-- bigger.

00:00:31.263 --> 00:00:34.133
But scientists want to know
if exploring digital worlds

00:00:34.166 --> 00:00:37.303
can change our brains
in ways that improve our ability

00:00:37.336 --> 00:00:39.605
to navigate the real world.

00:00:39.638 --> 00:00:42.642
To find out,
we've built a giant maze

00:00:42.675 --> 00:00:45.745
to test their theories
for the first time ever

00:00:45.778 --> 00:00:48.347
outside the world of computers.

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:50.383
And my job?

00:00:50.416 --> 00:00:53.186
I'm the lab rat.

00:00:58.290 --> 00:01:01.394
[theme music playing]

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:12.472
[vacuum humming, stops]

00:01:12.505 --> 00:01:15.341 align:start position:0%
Our brains have been
profoundly transformed

00:01:15.374 --> 00:01:17.510 align:start position:0%
by our interactions
with technology.

00:01:17.543 --> 00:01:19.879 align:start position:0%
A lot of the information
that I used to have to store

00:01:19.912 --> 00:01:23.249 align:start position:0%
in my brain
is now stored in my phone.

00:01:23.282 --> 00:01:25.351
My contacts, my schedule.

00:01:25.384 --> 00:01:27.153
In many ways, I've delegated

00:01:27.186 --> 00:01:29.322
what used to be done
by this organ

00:01:29.355 --> 00:01:32.658
to this new external organ.

00:01:32.691 --> 00:01:34.694
Doing that frees up
by brain's resources

00:01:34.727 --> 00:01:36.295
for other things that matter

00:01:36.328 --> 00:01:38.865
or that technology
can't quite do for us yet.

00:01:38.898 --> 00:01:43.236
So while we all don't have
implants in our brains yet,

00:01:43.269 --> 00:01:46.472
technology has already found
a way into our heads,

00:01:46.505 --> 00:01:49.475
which is why you may find it
deeply disturbing

00:01:49.508 --> 00:01:52.679
to see me do something
like this.

00:01:56.515 --> 00:01:57.483
Sh--

00:01:57.516 --> 00:02:01.320
[music playing]

00:02:01.353 --> 00:02:03.856
[Michael]<i> Studies show
 we can improve our brains</i>

00:02:03.889 --> 00:02:05.825
<i> by having enriching
 experiences,</i>

00:02:05.858 --> 00:02:08.427
<i> even by playing video games.</i>

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:10.329 line:0%
<i> To learn more about this,</i>

00:02:10.362 --> 00:02:12.465
<i>I came to UC Irvine's Stark Lab
 to speak with experts</i>

00:02:12.498 --> 00:02:15.301
<i> in the field of learning
 and memory.</i>

00:02:15.334 --> 00:02:17.170 align:start size:63% position:38%
So Dane and Craig,
you guys work on

00:02:17.203 --> 00:02:18.738 align:start size:63% position:38% line:0%
learning and memory.

00:02:18.771 --> 00:02:20.907 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
- What about them?
- So the lab is trying
to figure out

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:24.610 align:start position:0% line:0%
how memory works,
how it works in the brain.

00:02:24.643 --> 00:02:26.913 align:start position:0%
<i>And one brain structure, in the
temporal lobe</i>

00:02:26.946 --> 00:02:29.549 align:start position:0%
<i>that we know is important to
memory is</i>

00:02:29.582 --> 00:02:31.450 align:start position:0%
<i>the hippocampus.</i>

00:02:31.483 --> 00:02:33.953 align:start size:59% position:41%
So what does
the hippocampus do?

00:02:33.986 --> 00:02:35.655
We know it has a role in memory

00:02:35.688 --> 00:02:38.191 align:start position:0%
and, really, a certain
kind of memory.

00:02:38.224 --> 00:02:39.625 align:start position:0%
The hippocampus
is really involved

00:02:39.658 --> 00:02:44.330
when you need to rapidly form
new arbitrary associations.

00:02:44.363 --> 00:02:46.299 align:start position:0%
You know, remembering
what you did yesterday

00:02:46.332 --> 00:02:48.568 align:start position:0%
definitely needs
the hippocampus.

00:02:48.601 --> 00:02:50.670 size:88% position:44%
<i> Maybe we'll go the store,
 we park our car in the lot,</i>

00:02:50.703 --> 00:02:52.405 align:start position:0%
<i> and we need to be able
 to remember</i>

00:02:52.438 --> 00:02:53.706 align:start position:0%
<i> not just, "I parked
 my car in the lot,"</i>

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:57.643 align:start position:0%
<i> "I parked my car
 in this exact spot
 in the lot."</i>

00:02:57.676 --> 00:02:59.579
- [Michael]<i> Right. </i>
- [Craig]<i> And those details,</i>

00:02:59.612 --> 00:03:01.814 align:start position:0%
<i> that's what the hippocampus
 seems to be really be</i>

00:03:01.847 --> 00:03:02.915 align:start position:0%
<i> helping us out on.</i>

00:03:02.948 --> 00:03:04.951 align:start size:78% position:22%
And you keep looking down
at this piece of chewed

00:03:04.984 --> 00:03:07.353 align:start size:69% position:31%
bubblegum on the book,
is that a hippocampus?

00:03:07.386 --> 00:03:09.755 align:start position:0%
[Craig] Yes. This actually
is my hippocampus.

00:03:09.788 --> 00:03:11.490 align:start size:72% position:28%
Is this the whole thing
or is it symmetric...

00:03:11.523 --> 00:03:12.625 align:start position:0%
That's it--

00:03:12.658 --> 00:03:13.893 align:start position:0%
Oh, there's one
on the other side.

00:03:13.926 --> 00:03:14.994 align:start size:81% position:19%
That looks just like this?

00:03:15.027 --> 00:03:16.195 size:78% position:39%
Yeah, mirror image of it.

00:03:16.228 --> 00:03:18.364
[Michael]<i> In 2015,
 Dr. Stark and Dr. Clemenson,</i>

00:03:18.397 --> 00:03:19.832
<i> conducted a study to show</i>

00:03:19.865 --> 00:03:22.468
<i> how video games
 affect the brain.</i>

00:03:22.501 --> 00:03:23.769
<i> They gathered participants</i>

00:03:23.802 --> 00:03:25.504
<i> who normally didn't play
 video games</i>

00:03:25.537 --> 00:03:27.406
<i> and split them up
 into three groups.</i>

00:03:27.439 --> 00:03:28.707 line:0%
<i>A control group who didn't play</i>

00:03:28.741 --> 00:03:31.744 line:0%
<i> any video games for two weeks,
 an active control group</i>

00:03:31.777 --> 00:03:34.280 line:0%
<i> who played two-dimensional
 games for two weeks,</i>

00:03:34.313 --> 00:03:35.514 line:0%
<i> and an experimental group</i>

00:03:35.547 --> 00:03:37.650 line:0%
<i> who played 3D games
 for two weeks.</i>

00:03:37.683 --> 00:03:39.585 line:0%
<i> Beforehand, they had all
 the participants</i>

00:03:39.618 --> 00:03:42.255
<i> perform two virtual tasks
 on computers</i>

00:03:42.288 --> 00:03:43.856
<i> to measure
 their spatial memory.</i>

00:03:43.889 --> 00:03:46.492 align:start size:88% position:13%
As soon as they came back,
we re-administered
those two tasks.

00:03:46.525 --> 00:03:48.561
<i> And what we found was that the
 people who played the 3D game</i>

00:03:48.594 --> 00:03:50.696
<i> saw an improving
 in their test scores,</i>

00:03:50.729 --> 00:03:51.998
<i> whereas the control group</i>

00:03:52.031 --> 00:03:53.933
<i> and active control group
 did not.</i>

00:03:53.966 --> 00:03:55.534
<i> We didn't do brain scans,</i>

00:03:55.567 --> 00:03:57.536
<i> but we can speculate
 that there were changes</i>

00:03:57.569 --> 00:04:00.573
<i> to the experimental group's
 hippocampi.</i>

00:04:00.606 --> 00:04:03.876 align:start size:81% position:19%
So what are we going to be
doing to me here?

00:04:03.909 --> 00:04:05.811
So we're gonna do everything
that we've done before

00:04:05.844 --> 00:04:06.979
in our past studies,

00:04:07.012 --> 00:04:08.614 align:start position:0%
except we're gonna add
two new things.

00:04:08.647 --> 00:04:11.417 align:start position:0%
Uh, the first is we're gonna
add some brain scans,

00:04:11.450 --> 00:04:12.585 align:start position:0%
so we're gonna see
if we see a change

00:04:12.618 --> 00:04:14.420 align:start position:0%
in the structural side
of your hippocampus.

00:04:14.453 --> 00:04:16.555 size:84% position:42%
We've never actually looked
at somebody's brain scans

00:04:16.588 --> 00:04:18.491 align:start position:0%
before and after
they played video games.

00:04:18.524 --> 00:04:19.959
And the second thing
we're actually gonna do

00:04:19.992 --> 00:04:22.428
is we're gonna put you through
a real-world space.

00:04:22.461 --> 00:04:25.298 align:start position:0%
You're gonna be the rat
in a maze.

00:04:25.331 --> 00:04:27.833
[Michael]
<i> This truly untested territory.</i>

00:04:27.866 --> 00:04:30.436
<i> The effect of video-gaming
 on spatial memory</i>

00:04:30.469 --> 00:04:32.538
<i> has never been studied
 in a physical environment</i>

00:04:32.571 --> 00:04:35.308
<i> on a scale this big
 and comprehensive.</i>

00:04:35.341 --> 00:04:37.410
<i> I will have to navigate my way</i>

00:04:37.443 --> 00:04:39.912
<i> through a 3,600-square-foot
 physical maze.</i>

00:04:39.945 --> 00:04:43.649
<i> Will playing video games
 improve my mental skills</i>

00:04:43.682 --> 00:04:44.984
<i> in the real world?</i>

00:04:45.017 --> 00:04:47.320
<i> If so, society
 may start looking</i>

00:04:47.353 --> 00:04:49.422
<i> at gaming in a whole new way.</i>

00:04:49.455 --> 00:04:51.424 line:0%
<i> First, we had to get</i>

00:04:51.457 --> 00:04:53.759 line:0%
<i> baseline measurements
 of my brain.</i>

00:04:53.792 --> 00:04:55.361 align:start size:81% position:19%
Welcome to the MRI Center.

00:04:55.394 --> 00:04:57.596 align:start position:0%
We're gonna be taking a whole
series of scans of you,

00:04:57.629 --> 00:04:58.964 align:start position:0%
as the before scan

00:04:58.997 --> 00:05:01.334 align:start position:0%
to then see what's gonna be
happening to your brain

00:05:01.367 --> 00:05:02.802 align:start position:0%
as a function of actually
doing the gaming.

00:05:02.835 --> 00:05:04.737 align:start size:78% position:22%
Cool. What kind of things
are you looking for?

00:05:04.770 --> 00:05:06.906 align:start position:0%
Changes in the size and shape
of your hippocampus

00:05:06.939 --> 00:05:09.975 align:start position:0%
and also changes
in the connectivity
between brain regions.

00:05:10.008 --> 00:05:13.346
[Michael]<i> My brain was scanned
 using diffusion MRI,</i>

00:05:13.379 --> 00:05:14.613
<i> with a special emphasis</i>

00:05:14.646 --> 00:05:17.616
<i> on my all-important
 hippocampus.</i>

00:05:17.649 --> 00:05:18.884
[music playing]

00:05:18.917 --> 00:05:20.353 size:81% position:41%
[Craig]<i> So this first test</i>

00:05:20.386 --> 00:05:22.121 align:start position:0%
is a standard memory test
that we do.

00:05:22.154 --> 00:05:24.490 align:start position:0%
It's called an object
recognition memory test.

00:05:24.523 --> 00:05:26.425 line:0%
[Michael]
<i> This test began by showing me</i>

00:05:26.458 --> 00:05:28.961 line:0%
<i> a series of random objects.</i>

00:05:28.994 --> 00:05:32.865 line:0%
<i> I did my best to commit
 every one of them to memory.</i>

00:05:32.898 --> 00:05:33.899
Okay.

00:05:33.932 --> 00:05:35.534
- Finished.
- All right.

00:05:35.567 --> 00:05:36.769 align:start position:0%
What we're gonna do now though,
is we're gonna test

00:05:36.802 --> 00:05:39.739
- your memory for those objects.
- Okay.

00:05:39.772 --> 00:05:41.540 align:start position:0%
And this is actually
where it starts to tap

00:05:41.573 --> 00:05:43.509 align:start position:0%
into the hippocampus
that we know is so important

00:05:43.542 --> 00:05:44.910 align:start position:0%
for things
like spatial memory.

00:05:44.944 --> 00:05:48.147
 [Michael]<i> This time, I had to
 view another series of objects</i>

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:50.116
<i> and identify
 any that were identical</i>

00:05:50.149 --> 00:05:51.951
<i> to the ones
 I'd seen previously.</i>

00:05:51.984 --> 00:05:55.087
<i> The catch, some of the items
 were very similar</i>

00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:56.155
<i> to the earlier ones,</i>

00:05:56.188 --> 00:05:58.657
<i> but not exactly the same.</i>

00:05:58.690 --> 00:06:00.493
<i> This tested my memory
 for details</i>

00:06:00.526 --> 00:06:02.061
<i> and very slight changes.</i>

00:06:02.094 --> 00:06:03.362 size:78% position:39%
Okay.

00:06:03.396 --> 00:06:05.765
[Michael]<i> Next up, a virtual
 version of a water maze</i>

00:06:05.798 --> 00:06:08.134
<i> normally used by rodents
 and mice.</i>

00:06:08.167 --> 00:06:11.036 align:start size:75% position:25% line:0%
[Dane] The idea is that
you are trying to locate

00:06:11.069 --> 00:06:13.672 align:start size:59% position:41% line:0%
a hidden platform
in a pool of water.

00:06:13.705 --> 00:06:14.640 align:start position:0% line:0%
[Michael] Oh, man,

00:06:14.673 --> 00:06:16.909
I'm so glad I'm not a lab rat.

00:06:16.942 --> 00:06:19.779
<i>This task really put my spatial
 memory to the test.</i>

00:06:19.812 --> 00:06:22.815
<i> I had to find the same
 invisible underwater platform</i>

00:06:22.848 --> 00:06:24.583
<i> over and over again,</i>

00:06:24.616 --> 00:06:27.520
<i> using only the shapes
 of the mountains as my guide,</i>

00:06:27.553 --> 00:06:29.688
<i> but at least I didn't have
 to get wet.</i>

00:06:29.721 --> 00:06:31.023 align:start position:0%
Hey.

00:06:31.056 --> 00:06:32.725 align:start position:0%
That was more difficult
than I expected.

00:06:32.758 --> 00:06:35.528
[Craig] These are the sorts of
tasks that we've been able to do

00:06:35.561 --> 00:06:37.062 align:start position:0%
because we can put them
on a computer.

00:06:37.095 --> 00:06:38.464 size:88% position:44%
And we're gonna revisit them

00:06:38.497 --> 00:06:39.865 align:start position:0%
after you've done
the video games.

00:06:39.898 --> 00:06:43.102 size:81% position:41%
But we also have a really
great opportunity here now

00:06:43.135 --> 00:06:46.439
to be able to try to take it out
of just doing it on the computer

00:06:46.472 --> 00:06:48.441 align:start position:0%
and actually get it
into the real world.

00:06:48.474 --> 00:06:49.708 align:start size:56% position:44%
Have you guys done
this before?

00:06:49.741 --> 00:06:52.111 align:start position:0%
No. We don't get to do
this kind of thing.

00:06:52.144 --> 00:06:54.847 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Well, welcome
to the<i> Mind Field. </i>
- Awesome.

00:06:54.880 --> 00:06:57.750
[music playing]

00:07:00.619 --> 00:07:02.121
[Michael]
So this is it? It's huge.

00:07:02.154 --> 00:07:03.989 align:start position:0%
[Craig] This is what
we brought you here for,

00:07:04.022 --> 00:07:06.826 align:start position:0%
to have a real-world test
of memory.

00:07:06.859 --> 00:07:09.695
You're gonna be
a lab rat in a maze.

00:07:09.728 --> 00:07:10.996
So this is a big first for us.

00:07:11.029 --> 00:07:12.865
It's a big first really
for memory research.

00:07:12.898 --> 00:07:14.200 align:start size:59% position:41%
[Michael]
So how do you think
that'll effect

00:07:14.233 --> 00:07:16.635 align:start size:56% position:44%
what you guys
have already seen,

00:07:16.668 --> 00:07:18.871 align:start size:63% position:38%
which is
that moving around
in a 3D environment

00:07:18.904 --> 00:07:22.608 align:start size:63% position:38%
in a video game can
actually physically
affect your brain?

00:07:22.641 --> 00:07:24.877
[Dane] We would expect that
if we can somehow, kind of,

00:07:24.910 --> 00:07:26.912 align:start size:69% position:31%
train your hippocampus
to be better
at spatial memory

00:07:26.945 --> 00:07:29.215 size:78% position:61%
and spatial navigation,
we would see improvements

00:07:29.248 --> 00:07:30.116 align:start size:72% position:28%
in some of these areas.

00:07:30.149 --> 00:07:31.884 align:start position:0%
And it's not just
gonna be running a maze.

00:07:31.917 --> 00:07:34.520 align:start position:0%
You've got objects
embedded inside here,

00:07:34.553 --> 00:07:37.590 align:start position:0%
and we're gonna be testing
your ability to remember
where everything is.

00:07:37.623 --> 00:07:41.527 size:84% position:58%
Oh, and build a mental map
of whatever's inside there.

00:07:41.560 --> 00:07:42.661
[Craig]
So you have five minutes.

00:07:42.694 --> 00:07:44.230
Go on in, explore,
learn the maze,

00:07:44.263 --> 00:07:45.531
and learn the objects.

00:07:45.564 --> 00:07:46.799 align:start size:9% position:91%
Go.

00:07:48.066 --> 00:07:50.202
[Michael]<i> Because the walls
 were six feet tall,</i>

00:07:50.235 --> 00:07:52.838
<i> I was unable to get
 a bird's-eye view.</i>

00:07:52.871 --> 00:07:55.674
<i> My task was to create
 a spatial memory</i>

00:07:55.707 --> 00:07:59.078
<i> based entirely on the angles
 and turns of the white walls</i>

00:07:59.111 --> 00:08:00.546
<i> I could see at eye level,</i>

00:08:00.579 --> 00:08:02.715
<i> and a few tall trees
 and light poles</i>

00:08:02.748 --> 00:08:04.083
<i> outside the maze.</i>

00:08:04.917 --> 00:08:06.952
Okay. So I've oriented myself.

00:08:06.985 --> 00:08:08.220
The entrance is that way.

00:08:08.253 --> 00:08:10.222
There's an exit over there.

00:08:10.255 --> 00:08:13.058
I'm considering this the right
side, that the left side.

00:08:13.091 --> 00:08:15.227
I've got a vague idea
of where things are

00:08:15.260 --> 00:08:18.631
that I feel like exist
along the outside edge,

00:08:18.664 --> 00:08:21.800
but I don't know about
a lot of the stuff inside.

00:08:21.833 --> 00:08:23.669
[Craig] And time.

00:08:23.702 --> 00:08:25.871 align:start size:72% position:28%
All right. So
now you've had a chance
to explore the maze,

00:08:25.904 --> 00:08:27.940 align:start size:50% position:50%
find out where
the objects are.

00:08:27.973 --> 00:08:29.308 align:start position:0%
Now we're gonna test
your memory...

00:08:29.341 --> 00:08:30.276 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Okay.
- ...and we'll be timing you

00:08:30.309 --> 00:08:30.943
and seeing where you go.

00:08:30.977 --> 00:08:31.911
- Okay.
- Okay. You ready?

00:08:31.944 --> 00:08:33.312
- I'm ready.
- [Dane] So your first object

00:08:33.345 --> 00:08:34.647
is the bicycle pump.

00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:35.948
- Go.
- [Michael] Pump. Okay.

00:08:38.083 --> 00:08:40.786
[Michael] Pump was just
always making right turns,

00:08:40.819 --> 00:08:45.224
hugging the rightmost
part of-- pump.

00:08:45.257 --> 00:08:47.726
Ha ha! Yes. Easy. Okay.

00:08:47.759 --> 00:08:49.995
Now, I guess I do the opposite
to get out.

00:08:50.028 --> 00:08:53.933
Left side-- yup,
I think I should make this turn.

00:08:53.966 --> 00:08:55.034
There it is.

00:08:57.202 --> 00:08:58.604
- You asked for a pump?
- All right.

00:08:58.637 --> 00:09:00.973 align:start size:78% position:22%
Item two, the basketball.

00:09:01.006 --> 00:09:03.309
[Michael]<i> Later, Dr. Stark
 and Dr. Clemenson</i>

00:09:03.342 --> 00:09:05.010
<i> would evaluate my performance</i>

00:09:05.043 --> 00:09:07.580
<i> on how fast I was,
 the number of errors I made,</i>

00:09:07.613 --> 00:09:10.950
<i> and whether I took the most
 optimal route each time.</i>

00:09:10.983 --> 00:09:11.651
[Craig] And time.

00:09:11.684 --> 00:09:13.919 align:start size:47% position:53%
Third item
is the cat. Go.

00:09:13.952 --> 00:09:16.255
[Michael]
Here kitty, kitty, kitty.

00:09:16.288 --> 00:09:17.723 align:start position:0%
Got it.

00:09:17.756 --> 00:09:18.958 align:start size:69% position:31%
[Dane] The fourth item
is the pillow.

00:09:18.991 --> 00:09:20.793
[Michael]
Retracing my steps.

00:09:20.826 --> 00:09:22.261
- [Dane] The crayon. 
- [Michael] Easy.

00:09:22.294 --> 00:09:23.796
- [Dane] The book. 
- [Michael] Okay.

00:09:23.829 --> 00:09:24.964
[Dane] The boot.

00:09:24.997 --> 00:09:26.899 align:start position:0%
Last item
is the water bottle.

00:09:26.932 --> 00:09:28.768
- Go.
- [Michael] Water bottle.

00:09:30.902 --> 00:09:32.705
I think--

00:09:32.738 --> 00:09:33.706
by--

00:09:33.739 --> 00:09:35.341
Yeah, it was back here.

00:09:38.076 --> 00:09:40.947
Maybe on the other side
of this wall.

00:09:41.847 --> 00:09:44.016
No? Oh, shoot.

00:09:44.049 --> 00:09:46.285
Okay. Maybe it's down here.

00:09:46.318 --> 00:09:47.386
Oh, wait.

00:09:47.419 --> 00:09:48.854
That's-- no?

00:09:50.389 --> 00:09:52.157
That's the central cube.

00:09:52.190 --> 00:09:54.159
It was down some sort of...

00:09:54.192 --> 00:09:57.129
a long corridor like this
in this area.

00:09:58.363 --> 00:10:00.165
Oh, man.

00:10:00.198 --> 00:10:02.668 line:0%
[Michael]<i> Until this point,
 things had gone pretty well,</i>

00:10:02.701 --> 00:10:05.137 line:0%
<i> but now it felt like
 my hippocampus was failing me.</i>

00:10:05.170 --> 00:10:07.039 line:0%
<i> With most of the items
 now gone,</i>

00:10:07.072 --> 00:10:08.307 line:0%
<i> I couldn't use them
 for reference,</i>

00:10:08.340 --> 00:10:10.909
<i> and it was difficult
 to distinguish the differences</i>

00:10:10.942 --> 00:10:12.778
<i> between the various
 white corridors.</i>

00:10:12.811 --> 00:10:13.946
Oh, dang it.

00:10:13.979 --> 00:10:15.814
Oh, what about through--

00:10:15.847 --> 00:10:17.416
Got it.

00:10:17.449 --> 00:10:20.352 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Bottle coming up.
- [Craig] All right.
There you go.

00:10:20.385 --> 00:10:22.187 align:start size:91% position:9%
- [Michael] Got it. Nice.
- That one was a little
tougher, huh?

00:10:22.220 --> 00:10:23.789
[Michael]
Yeah, it was tougher.

00:10:23.822 --> 00:10:25.324
- So we found all eight objects.
- Yup.

00:10:25.357 --> 00:10:27.192
[Dane] Now, we're gonna make it
a little bit more difficult.

00:10:27.225 --> 00:10:28.894
So we're gonna move
onto the next phase

00:10:28.927 --> 00:10:30.195
and that's gonna be
from the other side.

00:10:30.228 --> 00:10:32.031 align:start position:0%
[Craig] Navigating
the maze in reverse

00:10:32.064 --> 00:10:35.200 align:start position:0%
will be an even bigger test
of your spatial memory.

00:10:35.233 --> 00:10:38.203
We're gonna give you
a list of four things to get,

00:10:38.236 --> 00:10:39.137 align:start size:78% position:22%
in order.

00:10:39.171 --> 00:10:41.440 line:0%
[Dane] So the first sequence
is the book,

00:10:41.473 --> 00:10:43.342 line:0%
the bottle, the crayon,

00:10:43.375 --> 00:10:44.443 line:0%
and the boot.

00:10:44.476 --> 00:10:46.278 line:0%
- Go.
- [Michael] Book.

00:10:46.311 --> 00:10:47.780
I think--

00:10:47.813 --> 00:10:49.048
That's the ball.

00:10:52.284 --> 00:10:53.052
Got it.

00:10:53.085 --> 00:10:54.887
Oh, yeah, bottle
was that hard one,

00:10:54.920 --> 00:10:58.457
but now I remember
which alley to go down.

00:10:58.490 --> 00:10:59.692
Perfect.

00:10:59.725 --> 00:11:01.960
Crayon.

00:11:01.993 --> 00:11:04.196
Boot. Don't want that.

00:11:04.229 --> 00:11:04.863
Here it is.

00:11:04.897 --> 00:11:06.899
Okay. Now, I need the boot.

00:11:06.932 --> 00:11:08.033
Oh, I just saw the boot,

00:11:08.066 --> 00:11:10.302
but how did I--

00:11:10.335 --> 00:11:12.838
Got it.
I'm done. I'm coming back.

00:11:14.139 --> 00:11:15.274
- Got them.
- [Craig] All right.

00:11:15.307 --> 00:11:16.341
So then the next four.

00:11:16.374 --> 00:11:18.010
[Dane] The pump, the pillow,

00:11:18.043 --> 00:11:19.912
the basketball, and the cat.

00:11:19.945 --> 00:11:21.881
- [Craig] All right. Excellent.
- [Dane] All right. Nice job.

00:11:23.315 --> 00:11:24.450 align:start position:0%
[Michael] Okay.
So that was really fun,

00:11:24.483 --> 00:11:26.485 align:start position:0%
but I can't be
the only subject.

00:11:26.518 --> 00:11:28.754 align:start position:0%
This experiment
could use a control.

00:11:28.787 --> 00:11:30.489 align:start position:0%
How else will we know that me,
enriching my life

00:11:30.522 --> 00:11:32.424 align:start position:0%
with daily
video game playing,

00:11:32.457 --> 00:11:35.360 align:start position:0%
really causes a change
in my spatial memory, right?

00:11:35.393 --> 00:11:37.129 align:start position:0%
Well, luckily for that,

00:11:37.162 --> 00:11:39.031 align:start size:50% position:50%
we've got a nice
matched control.

00:11:39.064 --> 00:11:40.466 align:start size:59% position:41%
Guys similar to me.

00:11:40.499 --> 00:11:41.934 align:start size:56% position:44%
Okay, one of them
has too much hair,

00:11:41.967 --> 00:11:43.368 align:start size:72% position:28%
but you guys look good.

00:11:43.401 --> 00:11:45.204
- You ready?
- [all] Ready.

00:11:45.237 --> 00:11:46.772 line:0%
[Michael]<i> In every experiment,</i>

00:11:46.805 --> 00:11:48.474 line:0%
<i> it's important to have
 a control group.</i>

00:11:48.507 --> 00:11:51.076 line:0%
<i> My look-alikes had to go
 through the exact same tests</i>

00:11:51.109 --> 00:11:52.778 line:0%
<i> as I did in the maze</i>

00:11:52.811 --> 00:11:54.413 line:0%
<i> to establish
 their individual baselines.</i>

00:11:54.446 --> 00:11:55.681 line:0%
<i> The difference would be
 that they would play</i>

00:11:55.714 --> 00:11:58.817 line:0%
<i> absolutely no video games
 for the next 10 days.</i>

00:11:58.850 --> 00:12:00.419 line:0%
<i> Then any change
 in my performance</i>

00:12:00.452 --> 00:12:03.388 line:0%
<i> would be compared against
 any changes in theirs.</i>

00:12:03.421 --> 00:12:04.957 line:0%
[music playing]

00:12:06.958 --> 00:12:09.161
<i> Next, I began
 my gaming regimen,</i>

00:12:09.194 --> 00:12:10.996
<i> starting
 from an ideal baseline,</i>

00:12:11.029 --> 00:12:12.898
<i> since I haven't played
 video games</i>

00:12:12.931 --> 00:12:14.199
<i> since I was a kid.</i>

00:12:14.233 --> 00:12:18.003
<i> Would ten days of gaming
 really make a difference?</i>

00:12:18.036 --> 00:12:20.039
[music playing]

00:12:28.246 --> 00:12:31.250
Technology isn't just affecting
the way we remember things.

00:12:31.283 --> 00:12:34.520 align:start size:81% position:19%
- [whirring]
- It's also playing
with the empathy

00:12:34.553 --> 00:12:37.456
and social circuits
of our brains.

00:12:37.489 --> 00:12:38.957
In fact, in many cases,

00:12:38.990 --> 00:12:41.126
we are more comfortable
relating to machines

00:12:41.159 --> 00:12:42.528
than we are to people.

00:12:42.561 --> 00:12:44.997
Just think about how much
we care about our phones.

00:12:45.030 --> 00:12:47.866
Roboticist
and MIT Media Lab alum,

00:12:47.899 --> 00:12:50.169
Alex Reben,
invented the BlabDroid,

00:12:50.202 --> 00:12:52.538
<i> a miniature robot equipped
 with a camera</i>

00:12:52.571 --> 00:12:54.106
<i> and an innocent little voice</i>

00:12:54.139 --> 00:12:55.474
<i> that asks
 very personal questions</i>

00:12:55.507 --> 00:12:57.409
<i> of unsuspecting pedestrians.</i>

00:12:57.442 --> 00:13:00.145
 [BlabDroid]<i> If you could
 take back one mistake,</i>

00:13:00.178 --> 00:13:01.413
<i> what would it be?</i>

00:13:01.446 --> 00:13:04.283
Oh, gosh.
I only get to take back one?

00:13:04.316 --> 00:13:05.951
[Michael]
<i> The majority of people</i>

00:13:05.984 --> 00:13:07.519
<i> instantly shared
 intimate details.</i>

00:13:07.552 --> 00:13:09.888
[BlabDroid]<i> Tell me something
 that you've never told</i>

00:13:09.921 --> 00:13:11.223
<i> a stranger before.</i>

00:13:11.256 --> 00:13:13.325
I'm scared
I won't be able to love

00:13:13.358 --> 00:13:18.063
and to let myself go
in a love relationship.

00:13:18.096 --> 00:13:19.231
[Michael]<i> In many ways,</i>

00:13:19.264 --> 00:13:21.333
<i> we are more comfortable
 talking to a machine</i>

00:13:21.366 --> 00:13:23.001
<i> than to a human.</i>

00:13:23.034 --> 00:13:25.571
But what about talking
through a machine?

00:13:25.604 --> 00:13:27.506
I mean, it's often easier to say

00:13:27.539 --> 00:13:29.608
difficult things
to a person via text

00:13:29.641 --> 00:13:31.443
instead of in real life,
isn't it?

00:13:31.476 --> 00:13:33.612
Well, what if the person
on the other end

00:13:33.645 --> 00:13:36.315
wasn't a friend
or a significant other,

00:13:36.348 --> 00:13:38.617
but was a therapist?

00:13:38.650 --> 00:13:41.153
<i> A mental healthcare startup
 called Talkspace</i>

00:13:41.186 --> 00:13:43.388
<i> allows adult users
 who pay a weekly fee</i>

00:13:43.421 --> 00:13:45.524
<i> to text therapists for advice.</i>

00:13:45.557 --> 00:13:48.026
[woman]<i> At Talkspace,
 we believe that therapy</i>

00:13:48.059 --> 00:13:50.128 line:0%
<i> should be anonymous,
 stigma-free,</i>

00:13:50.161 --> 00:13:53.899 line:0%
<i> simple, affordable,
 and comfortable.</i>

00:13:53.932 --> 00:13:56.134
Texting can give users
the distance they need

00:13:56.167 --> 00:13:57.502
to be open and honest.

00:13:57.535 --> 00:14:00.639
And messages can be sent
when the user wants,

00:14:00.672 --> 00:14:02.007
<i> not during an appointment</i>

00:14:02.040 --> 00:14:03.575
<i> or business hours only.</i>

00:14:03.608 --> 00:14:04.643
[woman]<i> Talkspace,</i>

00:14:04.676 --> 00:14:08.146
<i> therapy for how we live today.</i>

00:14:08.179 --> 00:14:09.481
[Michael] How am I?

00:14:09.514 --> 00:14:14.319
Better now that my phone
is working.

00:14:14.352 --> 00:14:19.391 align:start size:91% position:9%
Sometimes, however,
we aren't looking for
technology to comfort us.

00:14:19.424 --> 00:14:23.562
we're finding ourselves
wanting to comfort technology.

00:14:24.696 --> 00:14:27.032 align:start position:0%
This is a ROBOTIS OP2.

00:14:27.065 --> 00:14:28.634
Cute little fellow, isn't he?

00:14:30.168 --> 00:14:32.037
So how did that make you feel?

00:14:32.070 --> 00:14:33.071
Bad?

00:14:33.104 --> 00:14:34.273
Well, why?

00:14:34.306 --> 00:14:36.174
Robots are just machines,

00:14:36.207 --> 00:14:38.310
metal and wires
and computer chips.

00:14:38.343 --> 00:14:42.014
But we spend a lot of time
with technology.

00:14:42.047 --> 00:14:43.548
We depend on technology,

00:14:43.581 --> 00:14:45.117
and we care about it.

00:14:45.150 --> 00:14:46.685
But the degree to which
we empathize with it

00:14:46.718 --> 00:14:48.921
depends on context.

00:14:48.954 --> 00:14:52.591
[music playing]

00:14:52.624 --> 00:14:54.927
[Michael]
<i> Recently, my</i> Vsauce<i> office</i>

00:14:54.960 --> 00:14:56.595
<i> was invaded by bugs--</i>

00:14:56.628 --> 00:14:58.463
<i> robot hexbugs, that is.</i>

00:14:58.496 --> 00:15:01.133
<i> These bugs are made
 of plastic, metal,</i>

00:15:01.166 --> 00:15:03.001
<i> and electronic circuitry.</i>

00:15:03.034 --> 00:15:04.169
<i> They aren't alive.</i>

00:15:04.202 --> 00:15:05.537
<i> But could certain conditions</i>

00:15:05.570 --> 00:15:08.674
<i> cause them to inspire
 empathy in humans?</i>

00:15:08.707 --> 00:15:11.243
<i> A 2015 MIT study</i>

00:15:11.276 --> 00:15:14.079
<i> found that giving a robot
 movement, a name,</i>

00:15:14.112 --> 00:15:15.313
<i> and a personal backstory</i>

00:15:15.347 --> 00:15:17.449
<i> tends to increase
 its anthropomorphic effect,</i>

00:15:17.482 --> 00:15:19.484
<i> which can lead to
 an emotional connection</i>

00:15:19.517 --> 00:15:20.686
<i> with humans.</i>

00:15:20.719 --> 00:15:22.554
<i> We decided to see this in
 action.</i>

00:15:22.587 --> 00:15:25.557
- Thank you for your help today.
- Of course. Pleasure.

00:15:25.590 --> 00:15:26.691
[Michael]
<i> In our demonstration,</i>

00:15:26.725 --> 00:15:28.994
<i> our subjects think
 they're focus testing</i>

00:15:29.027 --> 00:15:31.129
<i>a new user-friendly technology.</i>

00:15:31.162 --> 00:15:34.132
<i> In this case,
they're given a lifeless hexbug</i>

00:15:34.165 --> 00:15:36.034
<i> and asked to describe it.</i>

00:15:36.067 --> 00:15:38.170 align:start position:0%
This thing kind of
looks like a bug,

00:15:38.203 --> 00:15:39.504 align:start position:0%
only I don't know
what it does.

00:15:39.537 --> 00:15:41.073
It has a switch on the bottom.

00:15:41.106 --> 00:15:42.140
It's light.

00:15:42.173 --> 00:15:43.275
It's sort of a rectangle,

00:15:43.308 --> 00:15:45.310
but the ends
are like hexagons.

00:15:45.343 --> 00:15:47.446
[Michael]<i> Then it was time
 to test their empathy.</i>

00:15:47.479 --> 00:15:49.114 align:start size:63% position:38%
Now, Karina,
what I would
like you to do now

00:15:49.147 --> 00:15:52.184 align:start size:63% position:38%
is place the item
in the middle
of that block.

00:15:52.217 --> 00:15:54.119 align:start size:63% position:38%
There's a magnet
that will hold it.

00:15:54.152 --> 00:15:56.055 align:start size:75% position:25%
And I would like for you
to take this mallet

00:15:57.355 --> 00:15:58.390 align:start size:63% position:38%
and please smash it.

00:15:58.423 --> 00:16:02.728
Yeah. Really? Okay.
This is cool.

00:16:02.761 --> 00:16:05.230
[Michael]<i> Our participants
 demonstrated no resistance</i>

00:16:05.263 --> 00:16:06.732
<i> to smashing
 this lifeless object.</i>

00:16:06.765 --> 00:16:10.402
<i> Many of them
 even seemed to enjoy it.</i>

00:16:10.435 --> 00:16:12.137 align:start size:50% position:50%
Do you feel bad
for breaking it?

00:16:12.170 --> 00:16:15.040
Not really.
I felt indifferent to it.

00:16:15.073 --> 00:16:17.142 align:start position:0%
Not really 'cause
it wasn't real. [laughing]

00:16:17.175 --> 00:16:18.443
Not really.

00:16:18.477 --> 00:16:20.479
[Michael]<i> While these subjects
 exhibited no empathy</i>

00:16:20.512 --> 00:16:22.280
<i> to the inanimate bugs,</i>

00:16:22.313 --> 00:16:25.017
<i>look what happened when we gave
 the exact same bugs</i>

00:16:25.050 --> 00:16:26.718
<i> names and movement.</i>

00:16:26.751 --> 00:16:28.787
This is Margaret. Okay.

00:16:28.820 --> 00:16:30.188 align:start size:75% position:25%
I'm gonna place Margaret
down here.

00:16:30.221 --> 00:16:31.723 align:start size:50% position:50%
I just want you
to take a moment

00:16:31.756 --> 00:16:33.425 align:start size:56% position:44%
to watch Margaret,
all right?

00:16:33.458 --> 00:16:35.627 align:start size:72% position:28%
And you could feel free
to pick her up.

00:16:35.660 --> 00:16:37.562 align:start size:81% position:19%
She's really well-behaved.

00:16:37.595 --> 00:16:39.464 align:start size:91% position:9%
- She's honestly one
of our favorites.
- [woman, laughing] Okay.

00:16:39.497 --> 00:16:42.534 align:start size:78% position:22%
So how would you describe
Margaret's personality?

00:16:42.567 --> 00:16:46.338
A little erratic right now,

00:16:46.371 --> 00:16:48.373
but I think if I pick her up,
she calms down.

00:16:48.406 --> 00:16:50.409
[Michael]<i> Notice
 how the subject has already</i>

00:16:50.442 --> 00:16:53.712
<i> anthropomorphized the object,
 referring to it as "she."</i>

00:16:53.745 --> 00:16:55.447
Maybe she feeds off
my energy.

00:16:55.480 --> 00:16:57.382 align:start size:28% position:72%
Could be.

00:16:57.415 --> 00:16:58.583 align:start size:66% position:34%
Go towards the light.

00:16:58.616 --> 00:17:00.085 align:start size:69% position:31%
Go towards the middle.

00:17:00.118 --> 00:17:01.486 align:start size:69% position:31%
[Michael] Do you think
Margaret likes you?

00:17:01.519 --> 00:17:03.688
Yeah, maybe that's why
she's doing this,

00:17:03.721 --> 00:17:05.257
and maybe when I go
like that,

00:17:05.290 --> 00:17:07.726
she doesn't act
all erratic.

00:17:07.759 --> 00:17:09.194 align:start size:41% position:59%
That's Aaron.

00:17:09.227 --> 00:17:10.562 align:start position:0%
Hi, Aaron.

00:17:10.595 --> 00:17:12.330 align:start size:47% position:53%
He can be a bit
of a pistol.

00:17:12.363 --> 00:17:13.832
- No way.
- Yes.

00:17:13.865 --> 00:17:16.068 align:start size:63% position:38%
It really depends on
who's holding him.

00:17:17.368 --> 00:17:18.703 align:start position:0%
[laughing]

00:17:18.736 --> 00:17:21.273 align:start position:0%
Oh, yeah.
He's got a lot of energy.

00:17:21.306 --> 00:17:24.076 align:start position:0%
Aaron, hi.

00:17:24.109 --> 00:17:26.144
[Michael] Now that you've
interacted with Eli a bit more,

00:17:26.177 --> 00:17:28.146
how would you describe
his personality?

00:17:28.179 --> 00:17:29.748
Probably he's just nervous.
He's scared.

00:17:29.781 --> 00:17:32.284 align:start size:78% position:22%
- He doesn't know
what's going on.
- Hey, Joe.

00:17:32.317 --> 00:17:33.819
[Michael]<i> Will these subjects
 be just as willing</i>

00:17:33.852 --> 00:17:35.720
<i> to smash their bugs?</i>

00:17:35.753 --> 00:17:40.158 align:start size:63% position:38%
Amy, I'm gonna place
Margaret right here,

00:17:40.191 --> 00:17:42.828 align:start size:63% position:38%
and then I
would like for you
to take this mallet,

00:17:42.861 --> 00:17:46.498 align:start size:50% position:50%
and I'd like you
to smash it.

00:17:46.531 --> 00:17:47.599
No.

00:17:47.632 --> 00:17:50.368
I don't wanna hurt it.

00:17:50.401 --> 00:17:53.505 align:start size:66% position:34%
Just take this mallet
and smash Aaron.

00:17:53.538 --> 00:17:56.675
[music playing]

00:17:58.510 --> 00:18:00.745 align:start size:63% position:38%
[Michael]
I'm gonna ask you
to take this mallet,

00:18:00.778 --> 00:18:02.748 align:start size:63% position:38%
and I'd like for you
to smash it.

00:18:05.216 --> 00:18:07.519 align:start position:0%
Smash it?

00:18:07.552 --> 00:18:08.653
Hit it?

00:18:08.686 --> 00:18:09.888 align:start size:41% position:59%
And, Chris...

00:18:09.921 --> 00:18:11.323 size:75% position:38%
You want me to kill Joe?

00:18:11.356 --> 00:18:12.457 align:start size:53% position:47%
