to us as we age.

00:23:20.765 --> 00:23:21.666 align:start position:0%
Oh, wow.

00:23:21.699 --> 00:23:23.134
That's about what you might see

00:23:23.167 --> 00:23:25.670 align:start position:0%
in someone
who's getting really old,

00:23:25.703 --> 00:23:27.539 align:start size:88% position:13%
- but they might go down
by ten points.
- Exactly.

00:23:27.572 --> 00:23:29.541
So the second one we did
was the virtual version

00:23:29.574 --> 00:23:32.677
of the water maze task,
and you actually performed

00:23:32.710 --> 00:23:34.512
30% better the second time
you did it.

00:23:34.545 --> 00:23:35.814 align:start position:0%
Hey, not bad.

00:23:35.847 --> 00:23:39.451 align:start position:0%
I could tell that I was
using better strategies.

00:23:39.484 --> 00:23:40.852 align:start size:88% position:13%
Yeah.

00:23:40.885 --> 00:23:43.455
- We also had the real maze.
- [Michael] Yeah.

00:23:43.488 --> 00:23:44.889 align:start size:63% position:38%
[Craig]<i> As you know,
 we made two mazes.</i>

00:23:44.922 --> 00:23:47.025 align:start size:72% position:28%
<i> Despite our efforts
 to try to equate them,</i>

00:23:47.058 --> 00:23:49.627 align:start size:84% position:16%
the second maze was
a little bit more difficult

00:23:49.660 --> 00:23:50.795 align:start size:63% position:38%
than the first maze.

00:23:50.828 --> 00:23:52.230 align:start size:53% position:47%
If we took a look
at things

00:23:52.263 --> 00:23:55.800 align:start size:63% position:38%
like how quickly you
got the objects,

00:23:55.833 --> 00:23:57.769 align:start size:78% position:22%
how many errors you made,
and we looked

00:23:57.802 --> 00:23:59.971 align:start size:75% position:25%
at the control subjects'
performance in
pre- versus post-.

00:24:00.004 --> 00:24:01.706 align:start size:56% position:44%
So on all of them,

00:24:01.739 --> 00:24:03.942 size:84% position:58%
<i> they actually got a little
 bit slower in maze two,</i>

00:24:03.975 --> 00:24:06.711 align:start size:72% position:28%
and all but one of them
made more errors.

00:24:06.744 --> 00:24:08.213 align:start size:63% position:38%
We took a look
at your performance.

00:24:08.246 --> 00:24:10.748 align:start size:69% position:31% line:0%
<i> You didn't get slower
 from maze one to two.</i>

00:24:10.781 --> 00:24:12.049 align:start size:78% position:22% line:0%
<i> You actually got faster.</i>

00:24:12.083 --> 00:24:13.618
- [Michael]<i> Really? </i>
- [Craig]<i> And you made the same</i>

00:24:13.651 --> 00:24:15.520 align:start size:72% position:28%
exact number of errors.

00:24:15.553 --> 00:24:18.490 align:start size:69% position:31%
So they don't improve,
and you did.

00:24:18.523 --> 00:24:19.757 align:start size:50% position:50%
<i> And even though
 this experiment</i>

00:24:19.790 --> 00:24:21.759 align:start size:59% position:41%
<i> had a small number
 of subjects,</i>

00:24:21.792 --> 00:24:25.263 size:88% position:56%
<i> the results are consistent
 with our virtual maze study</i>

00:24:25.296 --> 00:24:27.165 align:start size:72% position:28%
<i> with 70 test subjects.</i>

00:24:27.198 --> 00:24:29.067
- [Craig] All right.
- Thank you, video games.

00:24:29.100 --> 00:24:30.769 align:start position:0%
What about
inside my brain?

00:24:30.802 --> 00:24:32.971 align:start size:84% position:16%
 [Craig]<i> Inside your brain,
 it's a little tougher
 to really tell.</i>

00:24:33.004 --> 00:24:36.608 align:start size:75% position:25%
<i> We would expect
 that any effect of this</i>

00:24:36.641 --> 00:24:38.009 align:start size:69% position:31%
<i> is going to be small.</i>

00:24:38.042 --> 00:24:39.611 align:start size:78% position:22%
<i> I mean, we couldn't take
 your hippocampus</i>

00:24:39.644 --> 00:24:40.946 align:start size:75% position:25%
